  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Scattered showers and a few thunderstorms this\nevening and overnight, showers continue into Saturday early \nafternoon. Gusty winds tonight through Saturday.\n\nA cold front is progressing through the area, currently near a\nline from Hot Springs to Blacksburg to Boone, and is triggering\nsome scattered showers. Additionally, in advance of the front \nin the more unstable air within portions of the Piedmont, a \ncouple of thunderstorms have occurred. \n\nThe 500mb trough will cross the area tonight, and so expect \nshowers to linger during the overnight hours and through \nSaturday morning. As the main piece of energy moves to the east \nby the afternoon, showers will erode generally from west to \neast, leaving the rest of the day dry. Rainfall amounts have \nincreased, mainly for the Piedmont and VA Southside, so could \nnow see up to a half of an inch of rain for those areas, and \nbetween two to three tenths of an inch elsewhere.\n\nWind gusts will continue to increase, peaking during the \novernight hours tonight, and gradually diminishing by sunset \nSaturday, though shifting from the southwest to the northwest \nfollowing the frontal passage. Gusts will be between 25 to 35 \nmph, locally higher along the ridges. Make sure to secure any \noutdoor Halloween decorations.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Frost in the mountains Sunday morning.\n\nConfidence has continued to increase on the potential for areas of \nfrost to develop in the mountains by Sunday morning, as high \npressure behind the front settles over the Mid Atlantic with \nclearing skies and calmer winds. Highest confidence is over the \nwesternmost counties in the mountains, from Greenbrier County WV to \nWatauga County NC, where look to fall to or just below freezing. The \nelevated valleys will be even more susceptible to frost, as the\natmosphere decouples overnight. A Freeze Watch has been issued \nfor those locations for late Saturday night through Sunday \nmorning. Take steps now to protect cold sensitive vegetation. \nNot as high confidence in frost farther east, especially along \nthe Blue Ridge, as there may be enough wind to keep the \natmosphere mixed enough to keep temperatures above freezing. \n\nSunday night into Monday morning will be cold as well, but not quite \nas cold. Still cold enough to warrant a heavier coat or an extra \nlayer or two while waiting at the bus stop. After that, temperatures \nstart to moderate towards normal for the rest of the week.\n\nEarly next week, models are coming into consensus on a piece of \nenergy aloft becoming cut off from the main trough as it tracks\ntowards the coast, and a surface low developing along the front\nonce it moves offshore; however, there is still plenty of \ndisagreement in the eventual positioning of the upper cut off \nlow. While any impacts will be mainly limited to the coasts, \nguidance has trended towards increasing rain chances for the \nPiedmont and Southside for the early part of next week.",
